---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
const title = "Including Adults with Severe Intellectual Disabilities in Co-Design through Active Support";
---
<BaseLayout title={title}>
  <h1>{title}</h1>
  <p class="meta">My take on the paper.</p>

  <div class="paper">
    <p>
      The paper highlights the need for including adults with severe intellectual disabilities or through proxies in the design of products that support them well. The paper is great at showing how diverse the intellectual disability system is, and within that spectrum, there are unique differences that make a person an individual, and might be the reason why a product designed for a particular disability doesn't work for the target audience. Figure 1 is how I re-imagined Intellectual Disability and why we need to be more cautious while designing products, support, or even services for people who need them.
    </p>

    <figure class="figure">
      <img src="/figures/reading-4/figure-id-spectrum.png" alt="Figure 1: Multi-dimensional re-imagination of the Intellectual Disability spectrum, showing overlapping axes including Autism Spectrum, Behavioral dysregulation, Sensory, Non Verbal, Speech and Oral Motor, and Developmental Coordination Disorder" />
      <figcaption>Figure 1</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      The principle of Active Support System also highlights the diversity within intellectual disability, and I believe it can be further extended to other disabilities, particularly when researching, designing, or developing a community for people with disabilities. The 3rd principle, graded assistance, and the 4th principle of maximizing choice and control can prevent the over-medicalization of disabilities and give more control to the person, thereby improving the Experience-Based Design.
    </p>

    <p>
      The paper highlights the "doing for" and "doing with" difference and poses a real question of where to draw the line, and whether hand-over-hand is better or hand-under-hand for the people. And I think "fading" the support, not "diminishing" support, could be applied to this ethical conundrum. We forget that able-bodied people rely on each other for support more often than we think; we tend to notice more when it comes to people with disabilities, such as ID in this case, and often it is difficult to develop a system to support them, as the world is built with the proximity to the norm in mind.
    </p>

    <p>
      The future work could entail the present infantilization of people with ID (of all ages), the development of a framework to prevent this for caregivers, parents of children with disabilities, teachers, and healthcare providers. According to a workshop conducted on 22 October 2025, HCI and ASSETS have recognised the intellectual disability from the lens of accessibility, which is very much needed; however, there is a gap in the acknowledgement of intimacy among people with ID. People with ID or developmental disabilities have been reported to be victims of sexual abuse. This can be attributed to the reason we view them as a society, i.e., likely asexual, and a lack of sex education for children and adults with ID. [1]
    </p>

    <p style="text-align: center;">
      Reference:
    </p>

    <p>
      [1] Samantha Conde. 2020. Establishing a Serious Game on Relationship Boundaries for People with Developmental Disabilities. In Proceedings of the 22nd International ACM SIGACCESS Conference on Computers and Accessibility (ASSETS '20). Association for Computing Machinery, New York, NY, USA, Article 105, 1–3. https://doi.org/10.1145/3373625.3417079
    </p>

    <p style="text-align: center;">
      Citation:
    </p>

    <p>
      Filip Bircanin, Margot Brereton, Laurianne Sitbon, Bernd Ploderer, Andrew Azaabanye Bayor, and Stewart Koplick. 2021. Including Adults with Severe Intellectual Disabilities in Co-Design through Active Support. In Proc. of the 2021 CHI Conf. on Human Factors in Computing Systems (CHI '21). ACM, New York, NY, USA, Article 486, 1–12.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
